= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $f(N)=\alpha N^2/(\beta N^2+S)$. Its nonnegative <fixed points> are $N=0$ and, when $\alpha^2\geq4\beta S$,
$$
\boxed{N_\pm=\frac{\alpha\pm\sqrt{\alpha^2-4\beta S}}{2\beta}.}
$$
Indeed a positive <fixed point> satisfies $\beta N^2-\alpha N+S=0$. The <linear stability> multiplier is
$$
f'(N)=\frac{2\alpha SN}{(\beta N^2+S)^2},\qquad f'(0)=0.
$$
At a positive <fixed point>, $\beta N^2+S=\alpha N$, hence
$$
\boxed{f'(N_\pm)=1\mp\frac{\sqrt{\alpha^2-4\beta S}}{\alpha}.}
$$
For strict discriminant positivity, $N_+$ is attracting and $N_-$ repelling; the extinction state is superstable. At equality the positive point has multiplier one. Since
$$
f(N)-N=-\frac{N(\beta N^2-\alpha N+S)}{\beta N^2+S},
$$
it attracts from above and repels from below at equality. This is a semistable <fixed point>, whose one-sided behavior requires the nonlinear calculation.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The two positive <fixed points> merge when
$$
\boxed{S_c=\frac{\alpha^2}{4\beta},\qquad N_c=\frac{\alpha}{2\beta}.}
$$
Here $f'(N_c)=1$, $f''(N_c)\ne0$, and increasing $S$ decreases $f$, so this is a <saddle-node bifurcation> of the map. For $S<S_c$, the cobweb descends to zero below $N_-$, rises towards $N_+$ between the positive <fixed points>, and descends towards $N_+$ above it. Monotonicity of $f$ prevents a trajectory from overshooting across the attracting <fixed point>. Thus $N_-$ is an extinction threshold.

For $S>S_c$, $f(N)<N$ for every $N>0$. The iterates decrease to a nonnegative <fixed point>, necessarily zero. At $S=S_c$, initial states below $N_c$ tend to zero and those above tend to $N_c$. The <sterile-release population threshold> therefore combines bistability below the critical release with extinction above it.

\Image[2011-ecology.png]
{title=Cobwebs for sterile release and phase portraits for the two-species competition model}

