= Solution
{parent=i}

The term $ru$ is unrestricted growth of species $u$, and $-ru^2$ represents its density-dependent self-limitation, with scaled <carrying capacity> one. The interaction $-ruv$ suppresses $u$ in the presence of species $v$.

Species $v$ has intrinsic growth $v$ and suppression $-\beta uv$ by $u$. Thus both interspecific effects are harmful: this is a <competition model>, with no self-limitation term for $v$. Its unbounded growth when $u$ disappears is a consequence of that modeling choice. The parameter $r$ is the growth-time ratio, while $\beta$ measures the effect of $u$ on $v$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <equilibrium points> are
$$
(0,0),\qquad(1,0),\qquad\left(\frac1\beta,1-\frac1\beta\right).
$$
The third is biologically interior only for $\beta>1$, and coincides with $(1,0)$ at $\beta=1$. The <Jacobian matrix> is
$$
J=\begin{pmatrix}r(1-2u-v)&-ru\\-\beta v&1-\beta u\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At $(0,0)$ the <eigenvalues> are $r,1$, so it is a repelling node. At $(1,0)$ they are $-r,1-\beta$, giving a stable node for $\beta>1$ and a saddle for $\beta<1$.

At the interior equilibrium,
$$
J_* =\begin{pmatrix}-r/\beta&-r/\beta\\-(\beta-1)&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad\det J_*=-\frac{r(\beta-1)}\beta<0,
$$
so coexistence is a saddle. The formal equilibrium with negative $v$ for $\beta<1$ is not a population state; there its trace is negative and <determinant> positive.

At $\beta=1$ the boundary equilibrium is nonhyperbolic. To determine its behavior into the positive quadrant, put $x=u-1$. A local <center manifold> has $x=-v+v^2/r+O(v^3)$, so $\dot v=v^2+O(v^3)>0$ for small positive $v$. Thus it is unstable to introduction of species $v$, though attracting along the $v=0$ axis. The boundary/interior branches undergo a <transcritical bifurcation> at this parameter.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The positive quadrant is invariant. The non-axis <nullclines> are $u+v=1$ and $u=1/\beta$. Below $u+v=1$, $u$ increases; above it, $u$ decreases. To the left of $u=1/\beta$, $v$ increases; to the right, it decreases.

For $\beta>1$, the <stable manifold> of the interior saddle separates trajectories tending to $(1,0)$ from those for which $u\to0$ and $v\to\infty$. Exactly on that manifold, the trajectory tends to the saddle. For the illustrated $r=1,\beta=2$, the separating manifold is exactly $v=u$, since the vector field preserves that diagonal.

For $\beta<1$, every strictly positive initial state ultimately has $u\to0$ and $v\to\infty$. Indeed $\dot u\leq ru(1-u)$ implies $\limsup u\leq1$, so eventually $1-\beta u$ is bounded below by a positive constant. Then $v$ grows exponentially, forcing $u$ down. At $\beta=1$, a positive $v$ eventually drives $u$ below one; thereafter $v$ increases. A finite positive limit for $v$ would force $u\to1$, inconsistent with $\dot u\to-rv<0$, so the same exclusion outcome holds.

There are no interior <periodic orbits>: the <Bendixson-Dulac criterion> with multiplier $1/(uv)$ gives
$$
\frac\partial{\partial u}\frac{r(1-u-v)}v+\frac\partial{\partial v}\frac{1-\beta u}u=-\frac rv<0.
$$
The <phase portraits> in the preceding figure show the threshold geometry and the absence of a bounded attracting coexistence state. Initial states on the axes retain the corresponding one-species dynamics.

