= 4
{parent=2011/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Poincaré-Bendixson theorem> states that the omega-limit set of a forward orbit confined to a compact region of the plane, containing no <equilibrium points>, is a <periodic orbit>.

Put $x=R\cos\theta$, $y=R\sin\theta$. Direct calculation gives
$$
\dot R=R[1-H(\theta)R^2],\quad H=1+\tfrac12\sin^2\theta-\sin^4\theta,\qquad\dot\theta=1+R^2\cos\theta\sin^3\theta.
$$
Here $1/2\leq H\leq17/16$ and $|\cos\theta\sin^3\theta|\leq3\sqrt3/16$. Thus on the inner circle $R=\sqrt3/2$ the radial velocity is positive, and on the outer circle $R=3/2$ it is negative. In their closed annulus,
$$
\dot\theta\geq1-\frac{27\sqrt3}{64}>0,
$$
so there is no equilibrium. The theorem gives a <periodic orbit> in this trapping annulus.

It is asymptotically stable: the vector-field <divergence> is
$$
2-4x^2-3y^2\leq2-3R^2\leq-1/4
$$
throughout the annulus. Its nontrivial <Floquet multiplier> is $\exp\int_0^T\operatorname{div}F\,dt<1$. This proves **an attracting <periodic orbit> exists**. There can be only one there: two such nested orbits would enclose an annular region with zero outward flux, contradicting the strictly negative <divergence> via the <divergence theorem>.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A positive equilibrium must have $v=u=s$. Let
$$
D=\sqrt{(1-a-d)^2+4d},\qquad\boxed{s=\frac{1-a-d+D}{2}\in(0,1).}
$$
It is the unique positive root of $(1-s)(s+d)=as$. At this point the <Jacobian matrix> is
$$
J_* =\begin{pmatrix}L&-(1-s)\\b&-b\end{pmatrix},\qquad L=\frac{s(1-d-2s)}{s+d}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}J_*=L-b,\qquad\det J_*=\frac{bsD}{s+d}>0.}
$$
It is linearly asymptotically stable when $b>L$, and unstable when $0<b<L$. In either region it is a node or focus according as $(L-b)^2-4bsD/(s+d)$ is nonnegative or negative. At $b=L>0$ the linear <eigenvalues> are purely imaginary. If $L\leq0$, it is stable for every $b>0$.

The requested boundary follows from $1-d-2s=a-D$ and $s/(s+d)=(1-s)/a$:
$$
\boxed{b=L=\frac1{2a}(a-D)(1+a+d-D).}
$$

To prove a <limit cycle> exists in the unstable case, a compact trapping set must stay away from the singular boundary $u=0$. The following construction supplies it. Choose $M>1$ large enough to contain the positive equilibrium and the orbit being considered. On $u=M$, $\dot u<0$, and on $v=M$ with $u\leq M$, $\dot v\leq0$. Put $P=ue^{-v}$. Using $u+d\geq d$ and completing the square in $v$ gives
$$
\frac{\dot P}{P}=\frac{\dot u}u-\dot v\geq1-u-(a/d+b)v+\frac{bv^2}u\geq1-u\left[1+\frac{(a/d+b)^2}{4b}\right].
$$
Choose $\varepsilon>0$ so small that $\varepsilon e^M$ times the square bracket is less than $1/2$, and then choose $0<\eta<\varepsilon$. The set
$$
\mathcal K=\{u\leq M,\ \eta\leq v\leq M,\ ue^{-v}\geq\varepsilon\}
$$
is compact in the positive quadrant and forward invariant. On the curved boundary $P=\varepsilon$, $u\leq\varepsilon e^M$ and $\dot P>0$. On $v=\eta$, $u\geq\varepsilon>\eta$ gives $\dot v>0$. Its other boundaries were checked above. The constants can be chosen so the equilibrium is interior.

When $b<L$, the equilibrium is a hyperbolic repeller. Remove a sufficiently small outward-flow neighborhood of it from $\mathcal K$. This leaves a compact trapping region containing no equilibria. The <Poincaré-Bendixson theorem> gives a periodic omega-limit orbit of a nonequilibrium trajectory, hence a <limit cycle>. The analytic vector field excludes an accumulating family of periodic return-map zeros unless the whole local return map is the identity, which is incompatible with the repelling equilibrium and the nonperiodic approaching trajectory. This is the <positive trapping construction for the Leslie-Gower model>.

