= Solution
{parent=i}

A <periodic orbit> of least period $k$ is a list of distinct points $x_0,\ldots,x_{k-1}$ satisfying $f(x_i)=x_{i+1}$ for $i<k-1$ and $f(x_{k-1})=x_0$. Equivalently $f^k(x_0)=x_0$, with no smaller positive iterate fixing $x_0$. For $k=1$ this is a <fixed point>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Follow a perturbation through one complete circuit. The chain rule gives
$$
\delta x_k=M\delta x_0+O(\|\delta x_0\|^2),\qquad M=Df(x_{k-1})\cdots Df(x_1)Df(x_0).
$$
After $m$ circuits the linear perturbation is $M^m\delta x_0$. With distinct <eigenvalues>, the <eigenvectors> span over the complex numbers, and each component is multiplied by $\lambda_j^m$. Thus the <periodic orbit> is linearly asymptotically stable exactly when
$$
\boxed{|\lambda_j(M)|<1\quad\text{for every }j.}
$$
These are the orbit's discrete <Floquet multipliers>. Any modulus greater than one causes instability; a unit modulus prevents decay in the linear system, and nonlinear stability then needs further analysis. The finitely many intermediate <derivative> factors do not affect convergence to zero between complete circuits.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A period-$k$ orbit of a <Poincaré map> corresponds to a closed trajectory, hence a <periodic orbit> of the underlying flow, meeting the chosen section at the $k$ distinct successive return points. Its time period is the sum of the $k$ return times, not necessarily $k$ times a single common time. Stability of the return-map orbit describes transverse stability of that closed trajectory; the flow itself also has the neutral time-translation direction.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

There is always the <fixed point> $(0,0)$. For $P>0$, the first fixed-point equation gives $e^{-P}=1/r$, and the second gives $P=N(1-1/r)$. Thus for $r>1$ there is exactly one positive <fixed point>,
$$
\boxed{P_* =\log r,\qquad N_* =\frac{r\log r}{r-1}.}
$$
For $r=1$, every point $(N,0)$, $N\geq0$, is fixed. There are no other nonnegative <fixed points>.

At the origin the <Jacobian matrix> has <eigenvalues> $r,0$. It is asymptotically stable for $0<r<1$ and a saddle for $r>1$. For $r<1$, the bounds $N_{t+1}\leq rN_t$ and $P_{t+1}\leq N_t$ also prove global extinction in the nonnegative quadrant.

At the positive <fixed point>,
$$
J_* =\begin{pmatrix}1&-N_*\\(r-1)/r&N_*/r\end{pmatrix},\qquad\operatorname{tr}J_* =1+\frac{\log r}{r-1}<2,\qquad\det J_* =N_*>1.
$$
Here $\log r<r-1$ and $\log r>1-1/r$ for $r>1$. The discriminant is negative because the positive trace is less than two and <determinant> exceeds one. The conjugate multipliers have modulus $\sqrt{N_*}>1$, so **the positive <fixed point> is always unstable**. This is the <instability of the Nicholson-Bailey equilibrium>.

For completeness, on the $r=1$ fixed line,
$$
J(N,0)=\begin{pmatrix}1&-N\\0&N\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For $0\leq N<1$, linear perturbations are bounded but have a neutral component along the fixed line; there is no asymptotic stability of an individual point. For $N>1$ there is transverse instability. At $N=1$, the repeated unit <eigenvalue> has a nontrivial <Jordan block>, so powers have an unbounded off-diagonal entry and the linearized system is unstable. This degenerate case is not covered by a distinct-<eigenvalue> assumption.

= 6
{parent=2011/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For uniform nearest-neighbor bonds of strength $K_0$ on a ring with $N\geq3$, retain the normalization of the given model:
$$
\boxed{\dot\theta_i=\omega_i+\frac{K_0}{N}\bigl[\sin(\theta_{i-1}-\theta_i)+\sin(\theta_{i+1}-\theta_i)\bigr],}
$$
with indices interpreted modulo $N$. On an open chain the interior equations are the same, while
$$
\dot\theta_1=\omega_1+\frac{K_0}{N}\sin(\theta_2-\theta_1),\qquad\dot\theta_N=\omega_N+\frac{K_0}{N}\sin(\theta_{N-1}-\theta_N).
$$
For nonuniform bonds, replace each $K_0$ by the corresponding $K_{ij}$. A convention without the factor $1/N$ is obtained by rescaling the bond strengths; it is not silently substituted here.

For all-to-all coupling define the <Kuramoto order parameter>
$$
\boxed{re^{i\psi}=\frac1N\sum_{j=1}^Ne^{i\theta_j}.}
$$
It is the centroid of unit phase vectors: $r$ measures phase coherence and $\psi$ the mean phase. Multiplying by $e^{-i\theta_i}$ and taking imaginary parts gives
$$
\boxed{\dot\theta_i=\omega_i+Kr\sin(\psi-\theta_i).}
$$
Thus only the two components of the <Kuramoto order parameter> mediate interaction.

For the symmetric stationary continuum branch, choose the rotating frame with $\psi=0$. Oscillators with $|\omega|\leq Kr$ lock at $\theta=\arcsin(\omega/(Kr))$, choosing $\cos\theta>0$ for stability. Those with $|\omega|>Kr$ drift, with stationary phase density proportional to $|\omega-Kr\sin\theta|^{-1}$. Their real contribution to the order parameter vanishes, since the integral of $\cos\theta/(\omega-Kr\sin\theta)$ over a period is the change of a periodic logarithm. Their imaginary contributions cancel between $\omega$ and $-\omega$ because $g$ is symmetric.

The locked oscillators therefore give the real self-consistency relation
$$
r=\int_{-Kr}^{Kr}\sqrt{1-\frac{\omega^2}{K^2r^2}}\,g(\omega)\,d\omega.
$$
Set $\omega=Kr\sin\theta$ and divide by $r>0$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{1=K\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos^2\theta\,g(Kr\sin\theta)\,d\theta.}
$$
This is the <Kuramoto self-consistency equation> for partial synchronization. The incoherent state $r=0$ must be considered before this division. If $g$ is continuous at zero with $0<g(0)<\infty$, the branch meets incoherence at $K_c=2/[\pi g(0)]$.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $x=z$, $y=\dot z$. The system is
$$
\dot x=y,\qquad\dot y=x^3-x-Ay-y^3.
$$
Its equilibria are $\boxed{(0,0),(1,0),(-1,0)}$. At $(\pm1,0)$, the <eigenvalues> are $(-A\pm\sqrt{A^2+8})/2$, of opposite signs, so these are saddles for every $A$.

At the origin, the <eigenvalues> are $(-A\pm\sqrt{A^2-4})/2$. It is a <stable focus> for $0<A<2$, a stable node for $A>2$, an <unstable focus> for $-2<A<0$, and an unstable node for $A<-2$. At $A=\pm2$ it is the corresponding repeated-<eigenvalue> improper node. At $A=0$, <linearization> gives a center, but nonlinear damping matters: with
$$
E=\frac{y^2}2+\frac{x^2}2-\frac{x^4}4,\qquad\boxed{\dot E=-Ay^2-y^4,}
$$
small closed energy sublevels are invariant and $\dot E=-y^4$ forces convergence to the origin. Thus it is a weak attracting focus, not a nonlinear center.

**Local bifurcation.** Write $a=-A$. Averaging the radial equation near the origin, or eliminating nonresonant cubic terms, gives
$$
\dot R=\frac a2R-\frac38R^3+O(aR^3+R^5).
$$
The $x^3$ restoring term has zero average radial contribution, while $\langle y^4\rangle=3R^4/8$. Thus $A=0$ is a <supercritical Hopf bifurcation> as $a$ increases: a stable cycle appears for small $a>0$, with leading amplitude $R\simeq\sqrt{4a/3}$.

**Global changes.** Two further bifurcations must be retained. Define $a_h$ as the parameter where the upper unstable branch from $(-1,0)$ reaches $(1,0)$. Central symmetry supplies the lower connection in the opposite direction, forming a <heteroclinic cycle>. Define $a_f>a_h$ as the subsequent collision of an inner attracting and an outer repelling <periodic orbit>. The complete <phase portraits> for $|A|<1$ are:

* $0<A<1$: an attracting origin between the two saddles; no <periodic orbit>, since $\dot E<0$ off the zero-velocity axis.
* $0<a<a_h$: a repelling origin surrounded by one attracting cycle. Appropriate inward saddle branches approach that cycle; trajectories outside its basin escape the central potential well.
* $a=a_h$: the attracting cycle persists inside a repelling two-saddle heteroclinic loop.
* $a_h<a<a_f$: an inner attracting cycle and an outer repelling cycle coexist. The outer cycle separates the bounded oscillations from escape.
* $a=a_f$: the two cycles merge into a semistable cycle, attracting from its inside and repelling from its outside.
* $a_f<a<1$: no <periodic orbit> remains; generic nonstationary trajectories escape, with the saddle stable manifolds as exceptional trajectories.

Here escape means leaving the central well and becoming unbounded; it is not an additional finite equilibrium. The <softening Rayleigh-Duffing phase portrait> in the figure includes the two critical cases.

The return-map calculation underlying this classification is useful because the two global critical parameters are very close. On the section $x=0,y>0$, let $H_a(p)$ be minus the velocity at the next crossing of $x=0$, so a symmetric cycle is a root of $H_a(p)-p$. Any <periodic orbit> must have its extrema inside $-1<x<1$, since at a positive maximum $y=0$ requires $x^3-x<0$. Its index is one, so it surrounds the origin and neither saddle. Every such cycle is centrally symmetric: its reflected image cannot be a distinct nonintersecting cycle of equal enclosed area. Thus this half-return test detects all central cycles. A <fixed point> with $H_a'<1$ is attracting and one with $H_a'>1$ repelling; the full-return multiplier is $(H_a')^2$.

For the saddle connection, let $p_L(a)$ be the upper velocity at $x=0$ from the left saddle's unstable branch and $p_R(a)$ the upper velocity at $x=0$ on the right saddle's stable branch. While $y>0$, their shooting equation is
$$
\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{x^3-x}{y}+a-y^2.
$$
Comparison shows $p_L$ increases with $a$ and $p_R$ decreases. At $a=0$, energy dissipation gives $p_L<1/\sqrt2<p_R$. At $a=1$, the lower comparison curve $y=(x+1)/2$ on $[-1,0]$ gives $p_L>1/2$, while the upper comparison curve $y=(1-x^2)/2$ on $[0,1]$ gives $p_R<1/2$. The latter follows from
$$
\left[\frac{x^3-x}{y}+1-y^2-y'\right]_{y=(1-x^2)/2}=\frac{(1-x)(x^3+x^2-x+3)}4>0,
$$
and the stable-branch expansion $y=(1-x)-3(1-x)^2/4+\cdots$ near $x=1$. Hence there is a unique $a_h\in(0,1)$ with $p_L=p_R$.

At that connection the saddle <eigenvalues> are $\lambda_u=(a_h+\sqrt{a_h^2+8})/2$ and $\lambda_s=(a_h-\sqrt{a_h^2+8})/2$, so $|\lambda_s|/\lambda_u<1$. The loop is therefore repelling. In particular, the attracting Hopf cycle cannot simply disappear in that connection. Near the upper endpoint $p_R$, the half-return map has
$$
H_a(p)=p_L(a)-C[p_R(a)-p]^{\nu}+\cdots,\qquad0<\nu=|\lambda_s|/\lambda_u<1.
$$
For $a$ just above $a_h$, this produces a second, repelling <fixed point> near the endpoint, in addition to the attracting inner one. Continuing the two roots of $H_a(p)-p$ makes their collision criterion explicit:
$$
\boxed{H_{a_f}(p_f)=p_f,\qquad H_{a_f}'(p_f)=1.}
$$
This is a <saddle-node bifurcation of periodic orbits>, not a disappearance of the <equilibrium points>.

For a reproducible numerical check of the global sketches, event-based integration of this half-return map and its variational equation gives
$$
a_h\simeq0.33144619,\qquad a_f\simeq0.33147066,\qquad p_f\simeq0.68733571.
$$
These values are computed by shooting the stated equations; their precision is unnecessary for the requested sketches. They explain why a coarse parameter sweep can miss the two-cycle interval entirely. The three bifurcation types are therefore **Hopf at $A=0$, a two-saddle heteroclinic connection at $A=-a_h$, and a fold of cycles at $A=-a_f$**. There is no pitchfork of equilibria because their locations remain fixed for every $A$.

